  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how gene reactivation timing can emerge from both network-level regulation and local chromatin dynamics. At the top, it contrasts “trans” effects, shown as an interacting gene network, with “cis” effects, shown as intrinsic molecular events occurring directly at a gene’s chromatin site.\n\nThe main schematic follows a gene after inactivation. Once the gene is switched off, it does not reactivate immediately; instead, reactivation occurs after a variable delay distributed over time. This delay is depicted as a broad reactivation-time distribution, implying that different cells or loci reactivate at different times rather than at a single fixed point.\n\nBeneath the timeline, the figure breaks this waiting period into multiple underlying single reactions. Examples shown include factor association and chromatin remodelling at a nucleosome-like structure. Each of these elementary steps has its own time distribution, and together they produce the overall reactivation-time profile. The long blue bar with repeated arrows suggests a sequence of stochastic transitions or rate-limiting steps leading from the inactive to the active state.\n\nOverall, the message is that gene reactivation is a multistep, probabilistic process shaped by both broader regulatory networks and local chromatin mechanisms.",
